import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"ONNX as a second backend, in two directions","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"ONNX as a second backend, in two directions","type":"adr","date":"2026-10-15T00:00:00.000Z","status":"accepted","status_note":"**Accepted (2026-10-15) — direction 1 shipped, and the boundary\\nheld.**  `packages/catopt-onnx` implements `Sink` over 169 IR ops\\n(opset 18), verified through `onnxruntime` (219 tests; ~150\\nprograms round-tripped).  Pipeline round-trip:\\n`Linear→ReLU→Linear→softmax` 1.49e-08 (fp32), 3× stacked `Linear`\\n**1.11e-16** (fp64, machine epsilon), a tuple-returning two-head\\nmodule < 1e-6 on both outputs.\\n\\n**The falsification did not fire**: `git diff --name-only` against\\nthe package list shows **zero** changes under `catopt-core` (or any\\nother package) — the claim *\\"a new backend implements `Sink`;\\nnothing in core changes\\"* held on the first non-torch backend.\\n\\n**What the experiment actually found** (the valuable part):\\n\\n- Two port surfaces are thinner than a second backend needs, both\\n  worked around sink-side rather than by widening core: `Source`\\n  carries **no element type** for inputs (`Var.typ` is shape-only,\\n  so an index input is untypable without a dtype channel), and\\n  `Capabilities.ops` is spelled `torch_bindings` (cosmetic — the\\n  pipeline never reads it).\\n- `supported_ops` is a *name* set, but ONNX feasibility is\\n  sometimes **attribute-dependent** (a `transpose` needs a static\\n  rank); those refuse at lowering rather than at pricing time, so\\n  extraction can in principle commit to a form the sink then\\n  declines — no occurrence in the tests, but a real seam.\\n- Two attribute-spelling traps, exactly as predicted: ONNX\\n  `HardSigmoid` defaults `alpha=0.2` where ATen is `1/6` (silent\\n  20% slope error), and `Flatten` is always 2-D.\\n- fp64 is exact only where onnxruntime ships a double kernel\\n  (measured gaps: `Tan`, `Asin`, `Acos`, `Atan`, `Sinh`, `Cosh`,\\n  `Erf`, `Conv`, `InstanceNormalization`).\\n- Only `Monolithic` is exercised: `Compositional` needs a\\n  `Composer`, `Autotuned` a `Meter` — both torch-shaped ports with\\n  no ONNX equivalent.  **That is the datum for direction 2.**\\n\\n**Open follow-ups:** a staged coverage climb for `catopt-onnx`\\n(not yet in `[tool.coverage.run] source`); direction 2\\n(`OnnxSource`) remains unbuilt."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/adrs/0006-onnx-backend.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/adrs/0006-onnx-backend.md"}'),a={name:`graphopt/project/adrs/0006-onnx-backend.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t('<h1 id="adr-0006-—-onnx-as-a-second-backend-in-two-directions" tabindex="-1">ADR 0006 — ONNX as a second backend, in two directions <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0006-—-onnx-as-a-second-backend-in-two-directions" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR 0006 — ONNX as a second backend, in two directions”">​</a></h1><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Adopt ONNX as a backend, staged: export first, import second.</strong> Core does not change; ONNX lands as its own package behind the existing ports, with the op-mapping work made explicit rather than discovered late.</p><h3 id="direction-1-—-export-ir-→-onnx-—-the-first-stage" tabindex="-1">Direction 1 — export (IR → ONNX) — the first stage <a class="header-anchor" href="#direction-1-—-export-ir-→-onnx-—-the-first-stage" aria-label="Permalink to “Direction 1 — export (IR → ONNX) — the first stage”">​</a></h3><p><code>OnnxSink</code> implements <code>Sink</code>: IR → an ONNX graph (via <code>onnx</code> / <code>onnxruntime</code> for verification). This is the <em>inverse</em> of what <code>torch_bridge</code> already does — a table from the IR op set to ONNX ops plus the attribute spelling each op uses.</p><p>Value: an optimized program can be delivered to any ONNX runtime — the hardware-vendor compilers, mobile, non-torch serving — which is where the <em>measured</em> wins could actually reach users. Cost: moderate. The op table is finite and the IR is already the intermediate form; the risk is attribute spelling (see the positional-attr trap below), not architecture.</p><h3 id="direction-2-—-import-onnx-→-ir-—-the-second-stage" tabindex="-1">Direction 2 — import (ONNX → IR) — the second stage <a class="header-anchor" href="#direction-2-—-import-onnx-→-ir-—-the-second-stage" aria-label="Permalink to “Direction 2 — import (ONNX → IR) — the second stage”">​</a></h3><p><code>OnnxSource</code> implements <code>Source</code>: an ONNX graph → IR. This is what lets the optimizer touch models that are not torch at all.</p><p>Cost: <strong>the large one</strong>, for three reasons measured on the torch side:</p><ol><li><strong>The op-mapping long tail.</strong> The torch backend needed ~260 bindings, an <code>ATTR_SCHEMA</code> for positional→named attributes, and per-op shape rules. ONNX needs the same three artifacts for its op set — mechanical, but the long tail is where the bugs live: the U²-Net trial found <code>max_pool2d</code> silently truncating because <code>ceil_mode</code> arrives <em>positionally</em>, and <code>upsample_bilinear2d</code> placing <code>align_corners</code> at position 2 so the scale lands at 3 (committed <code>ecb2fb7</code>).</li><li><strong>Shape inference.</strong> The IR&#39;s typing is explicit; ONNX graphs carry less of it, and several laws (the carrier/<code>_vshape</code> family) are shape-guarded. Without a shape source the interesting half of the library is unreachable.</li><li><strong>Opset drift.</strong> ONNX ops change semantics across opsets; the mapping must pin the opset it claims to support.</li></ol><h3 id="prerequisite-already-met-multi-output" tabindex="-1">Prerequisite already met: multi-output <a class="header-anchor" href="#prerequisite-already-met-multi-output" aria-label="Permalink to “Prerequisite already met: multi-output”">​</a></h3><p>ONNX graphs are natively multi-output, and until this week the torch path silently delivered <strong>one</strong> output (<code>export_to_ir</code> took the first resolvable leaf and <code>break</code>ed; measured on U²-Net: 7 outputs, 1 delivered). The <code>pack</code> op + <code>IR.roots</code> + per-output verification (branch <code>feat/multi-output</code>) close that — a second backend would otherwise have inherited the same bug.</p><h3 id="the-interim-refuse-do-not-drop" tabindex="-1">The interim: refuse, do not drop <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-interim-refuse-do-not-drop" aria-label="Permalink to “The interim: refuse, do not drop”">​</a></h3><p>Until <code>OnnxSink</code> exists, the honest behaviour is what ships today: an explicit <code>unsupported export format</code> error. A silent single-output delivery is worse than a refusal, and the same rule applies to any ONNX op the mapping cannot express — decline the model, never drop a node.</p><h2 id="why" tabindex="-1">Why <a class="header-anchor" href="#why" aria-label="Permalink to “Why”">​</a></h2><p>Every model the optimizer has touched entered through <code>TorchSource</code> (<code>torch.export</code> → IR) and left through <code>TorchSink</code> (IR → <code>IRModule</code>). The hexagonal boundary was built for more than one backend — <code>AGENTS.md</code> states it outright: <em>&quot;A new backend implements <code>Sink</code>; nothing in <code>catopt-core</code> changes.&quot;</em> — and <code>import-linter</code> enforces it (core imports no torch, numpy, or GPU library; adapters push state into core through registered seams).</p><h2 id="not-doing" tabindex="-1">Not doing <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-doing" aria-label="Permalink to “Not doing”">​</a></h2><ul><li>none recorded — legacy record; the design doc lists what was deferred.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>The boundary is validated by use.</strong> The first non-torch</li><li><strong>Verification semantics must be pinned.</strong> The equivalence</li><li><strong>Costing stays backend-aware.</strong> `backend_cost(cost_fn,</li><li><strong>The staged order is a commitment.</strong> Export-first is not</li><li><strong>What this is not.</strong> Not a rewrite, not a new IR, not a</li></ul><h2 id="enforcement" tabindex="-1">Enforcement <a class="header-anchor" href="#enforcement" aria-label="Permalink to “Enforcement”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Code and tests are named in the design doc.</li></ul><h2 id="design" tabindex="-1">Design <a class="header-anchor" href="#design" aria-label="Permalink to “Design”">​</a></h2><p>The full record (context, tables, phases, trade-offs): <a href="./../design/0006-onnx-backend.html"><code>design/0006-onnx-backend.md</code></a>.</p>',24)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};